{
 "id": "2022-sun-chinese",
 "name": "Chinese Garden Rain Soundscape: Video With vs Without Sound (SD rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Sun & Dong (2022), \"Impact of the Classic Chinese Garden Soundscape With Focus on Physiological and Psychological Effects, Tested Through Eye-Tracking, and Subjective Evaluation\", Frontiers in Psychology 13: 902630. Participants watch the same one-minute video of rain falling on banana (Musa basjoo) leaves and the pond at Tingyuxuan (\"Listening to the Sound of Rain Hall\") in the Humble Administrator's Garden, Suzhou, twice: once with sound and once muted, in random order. After each viewing they rate the scene on the paper's 17 semantic-differential pairs (like–dislike, comfortable–uncomfortable, familiar–unfamiliar, happy–sad, relieved–unrelieved, calm–agitated, natural–artificial, monotonous–varied, light–dark, tidy–messy, warm–cold, static–active, relax–tense, classic–modern, harmonious–discordant, pleasant–unenjoyable, unique–ordinary) from −2 to +2. Answers are keyed \"with_sound\" / \"muted\" for a paired comparison; the page also records the order and whether the participant has visited the garden. Original result (24 students, Jiaxing; never visited the garden): with sound the scene felt significantly more familiar, calmer, more varied, brighter, warmer, less static and more pleasant (Hedges' g 0.46–0.76), and eye tracking showed more attention on the banana leaves. Video: the paper's Supplementary Video 1 (CC BY 4.0). Differences from the original study: (1) online on the participant's own screen and speakers or headphones, instead of a 22-inch monitor and speakers in an acoustic laboratory with the rain sound set to about 50 dB; the recording's sound level was raised by 20 dB so it is audible at normal device volume, and participants set a natural-sounding level themselves; (2) the video is cut to its first 60 s (the supplied file is 73 s); (3) no eye tracking and no physiological measures; (4) eyes-closed rest before each video is omitted; (5) age, gender and the garden-visit question are asked on the page instead of being screening criteria.",
